<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malay Annals</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sejarah Melayu, Sulalatus Salatin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malay Annals are a Malay literary chronicle of the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, tracing them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sang-nila-utama">Sang Nila Utama</a>'s founding of Singapura to the Portuguese conquest of Malacca in 1511. The best-known version was edited in 1612 at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>. Mixing legend with history, they are a main source for the early Malay kingdoms and for Malay ideas of kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malay-annals.jpg" alt="Malay Annals" width="245" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispiece_of_a_Jawi_edition_of_the_Malay_Annals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The original title is Sulalatus Salatin, the Genealogy of Kings. The introduction of the oldest version says it derives from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> called the Hikayat Melayu, perhaps dating from the time of the Malacca Sultanate, which the last sultan, Mahmud Shah, took with him when he fled to Kampar in 1511. Portuguese soldiers seized it in their attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a> in 1536 and took it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, and a nobleman, Orang Kaya Sogoh, brought it back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>; the historian Abdul Samad Ahmad suggests it came from Gowa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> instead, and William Linehan from Gua in Pahang.</p><p>On 13 May 1612, under Sultan Alauddin Riayat Shah III, the regent Raja Abdullah commissioned the bendahara Tun Sri Lanang to rewrite and compile it. When the Aceh Sultanate sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-sawar">Batu Sawar</a> in 1613 and carried the court into exile, Tun Sri Lanang finished the work in captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Written in Classical Malay in Jawi script, the work opens in praise of God, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and his companions and traces the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> to Raja Iskandar Zulkarnain. Its stories include Sang Sapurba's oath with Demang Lebar Daun of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, the strongman Badang, the fall of Singapura to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> after Sri Iskandar Shah wrongly punished a concubine, the founding of Malacca, the career of the bendahara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, Hang Tuah and the fairy princess of Mount Ophir. Its account of the Portuguese conquest differs from Portuguese records, which say the siege lasted 15 days and that Ahmad Shah died in battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The chronicles carried the Malaccan tradition onward and enshrined the sanctity of the ruler, or daulat, legitimising the absolutist governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>'s successor states, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> used them to claim to be the centre of Malay culture. Raffles MS no.18, acquired by Sir Stamford Raffles, is considered the oldest and most faithful version; John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leyden</a>'s English translation of it appeared in 1821, and Richard Olaf Winstedt's in 1938. More than 30 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive, and in 2001 the work was listed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World International Register.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malay Annals” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Malay chronicle <b>Sejarah Melayu</b>, the rulers of the Malacca sultanate claimed descent from the kings of the Chola empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its court preserved Malacca's royal genealogy, regalia and literature: the <b>Sejarah Melayu</b> (Malay Annals), the classic chronicle of the Malacca dynasty, is generally thought to have been revised for the Johor court in 1612, and the Bugis-Malay court of Riau-Lingga became a centre of Malay letters in the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Malay Annals</b>, a prince of Palembang, Seri Teri Buana, who claimed descent from Alexander the Great, landed on Temasek in 1299 and was made king of Singapura by the Orang Laut, the sea people once loyal to Srivijaya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers adopted Islam, though Malay sources such as the <b>Malay Annals</b> describe the people as raiders who challenged Malacca at sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/aru-kingdom">Aru Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Malay Annals</b> tell how Sang Nila Utama, a prince of Palembang, landed on the island of Temasek, saw a beast he took for a lion and named the place Singapura, lion city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-singapura">Kingdom of Singapura</a></li></ul></section>
